===Determiner===
{{en-det}}

# Each of the two; one and the [[other]]; {{non-gloss|referring to two individuals or items}}.
#: {{ux|en|'''Both''' (the/my) children are such dolls.}}
#: {{ux|en|Which one do you need? ―I need '''both''' of them.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Genesis|21|27||Abraham took sheep and oxen, and gave them unto Abimelech; and '''both''' of them made a covenant.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1717|author={{w|Henry St John, 1st Viscount Bolingbroke|Viscount Bolingbroke}}|title=Reflexions upon Exile
|passage=He will not bear the loss of his rank, because he can bear the loss of his estate; but he will bear '''both''', because he is prepared for both.}}

====Usage notes====
This word does not come between a possessive and its head noun.  Say ''both (of) my hands'',  not *''my both hands''.  Say, ''both (of) the king's horses'', not *''the king's both horses''.

